Here's a title block in LaTeX (article class), which you can adapt to your needs:

```latex
\documentclass[11pt]{article}

\usepackage{authblk}
\usepackage{hyperref}

\title{Adaptive Sparse Attention for Efficient Long-Context\\Language Modeling}

\author[1]{Alice M.\ Rivera}
\author[2]{Daniel K.\ Okafor}

\affil[1]{Department of Computer Science, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA}
\affil[2]{School of Informatics, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh EH8 9AB, United Kingdom}

\date{October 15, 2026}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Abstract text goes here.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} sparse attention, long-context modeling, language models, computational efficiency, transformers

\end{document}
```

**Rendered appearance:**

---

**Adaptive Sparse Attention for Efficient Long-Context Language Modeling**

Alice M. Rivera¹ and Daniel K. Okafor²

¹ *Department of Computer Science, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305, USA*
² *School of Informatics, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh EH8 9AB, United Kingdom*

October 15, 2026

**Keywords:** sparse attention, long-context modeling, language models, computational efficiency, transformers

---

**Notes:**
- The `authblk` package handles multiple authors and affiliations cleanly; the optional `[1]`/`[2]` arguments link each author to their affiliation.
- To add corresponding-author emails, use `\author[1]{Alice M.\ Rivera\thanks{Email: ...}}`.
- If you need a specific journal or conference format (IEEE, ACM, Springer, etc.), let me know and I can adapt the block to that template.

